// Worked example answers (one or two per city) plus budgeting — shown as extra lessons.
const MODEL = [
  { lessons: [
    ['Model answer: "Why management?"', '*Q: Why do you want to be an engineering manager?*\n*Answer:* "Over the last two years as a tech lead I noticed my best weeks weren\'t the ones where I shipped the most code — they were when I unblocked three people or helped a junior engineer lead their first feature. When our manager left for four months, I ran the 1:1s and the hiring loop. We hired two engineers who are now strong performers, and the team hit every quarterly goal. I want to keep doing that full time: grow people, build a healthy team and make sure we work on the right things. I still enjoy the technical side, so I stay close through design reviews, but I measure myself by the team\'s results."\n*Why it works:* real evidence, clear motivation, a measurable result, and a realistic view of staying technical.'],
    ['Model answer: "Tell me about a failure"', '*Q: Tell me about a time you failed as a leader.*\n*S:* As a new manager, I took over a team mid-way through a payments migration.\n*T:* I needed to land it by the end of the quarter.\n*A:* I assumed the plan was sound and didn\'t dig into the risks. Two months in, we found a dependency on another team\'s API that wasn\'t ready. I told my director as soon as I knew, laid out three options and we chose to cut two low-value features.\n*R:* We shipped three weeks late, with no customer impact.\n*Reflection:* Now, in my first two weeks on any project, I run a risk review with the team and list dependencies with owners. On my next project that found a blocker in week one."'],
  ] },
  { lessons: [
    ['Model answer: building a team', '*Q: Tell me about building a team from scratch.*\n*S:* We were launching a mobile app and had no mobile engineers.\n*T:* Hire five engineers in two quarters while keeping a high bar.\n*A:* I wrote a headcount plan with product: a senior mobile lead first, then two mid-level, then two juniors once we had mentors. I defined competencies for each level, built a four-round structured loop with rubrics and trained six interviewers. Written feedback went in before every debrief. I sourced through meetups and outreach, not just referrals, and removed "nice to have" requirements from the job ad.\n*R:* We hired five people in five months, with an 83% offer acceptance rate. All five were still on the team a year later, and the team was 40% women, up from 0%.\n*Reflection:* I\'d start onboarding docs earlier — the first two hires spent weeks finding information."'],
  ] },
  { lessons: [
    ['Model answer: hard feedback', '*Q: Tell me about the hardest feedback you\'ve given.*\n*S:* A senior engineer was technically excellent, but in design reviews he dismissed juniors\' ideas, and two of them stopped speaking up.\n*T:* Change the behaviour without losing his strengths.\n*A:* I met him privately the same week and used SBI: "In Tuesday\'s review (situation), when Priya suggested caching, you said \'that\'s obviously wrong\' and moved on (behaviour). She hasn\'t spoken in a review since, and we\'re losing ideas (impact)." He was surprised and a bit defensive, so I listened, then we agreed he\'d ask one question before critiquing an idea. I checked in every two weeks.\n*R:* Within a month juniors were contributing again. He later mentored Priya on her first design doc and listed mentoring in his promotion case.\n*Reflection:* I should have raised it after the first time, not the third."'],
  ] },
  { lessons: [
    ['Model answer: an underperformer', '*Q: Tell me about managing a low performer.*\n*S:* An engineer\'s tickets were taking two to three times longer than peers\', and code reviews often bounced back.\n*T:* Find the cause and either get him to the expected level or make a fair decision.\n*A:* In our 1:1, I shared specific examples and asked what was going on. It turned out he\'d moved from backend and was struggling with our frontend stack — a *skill* issue, not motivation. I wrote down clear expectations for his level, paired him with a strong frontend engineer for a month and set smaller, well-defined tasks. We reviewed progress every week, and I kept HR informed in case it became formal.\n*R:* After eight weeks his cycle time matched the team\'s, and six months later he owned a feature end to end.\n*Reflection:* Diagnose before you act — a PIP would have been the wrong tool."'],
    ['Model answer: letting someone go', '*Q: Tell me about a time you had to let someone go.*\n*A (summary):* "I gave clear feedback over two months with examples and support, then moved to a 60-day PIP with three measurable goals, agreed with HR. She met one of the three. I made the decision with HR, kept the conversation short, kind and private, and explained the severance and support. Then I told the team only that she had left and what would happen to her work, and I took over the most critical items. In my next 1:1s I checked how people were feeling. What I learned: I should have started documenting earlier — it would have made the process faster and fairer for her."'],
  ] },
  { lessons: [
    ['Model answer: planning a quarter', '*Q: Walk me through how you plan a quarter.*\n*Answer:* "Three weeks before the quarter, I collect inputs: company OKRs, product priorities, our reliability and tech-debt list, and asks from other teams. Then I work out capacity — for example 6 engineers × 60 days = 360 days, minus holidays and on-call, times a 65% focus factor gives about 210 days. With my PM and tech lead we size items in t-shirt sizes and rank them with RICE and cost of delay. We commit to about 75% of capacity, keep around 15–20% for tech debt, and mark the rest as stretch. I list dependencies with owners and dates, review the plan with stakeholders, and publish what we\'re *not* doing. Mid-quarter we check progress and re-plan if priorities change. Last quarter we delivered 5 of 6 committed goals, and the miss was a dependency we\'d flagged in week one, so nobody was surprised."'],
    ['Model answer: a project that slipped', '*Q: Tell me about a project that was going to miss its deadline.*\n*S:* A billing-system launch was tied to a contract date.\n*T:* Deliver without burning out the team.\n*A:* In week four our burn-up chart showed we\'d land three weeks late. I told my director and the PM that day with three options: cut the reporting module to a later release, move the date, or borrow an engineer from a sister team (only useful because it was early). We cut reporting and borrowed one engineer for the integration work.\n*R:* We launched on time, reporting followed four weeks later, and the customer was happy because we told them early.\n*Reflection:* Since then I track a burn-up chart from day one on every fixed-date project."'],
  ] },
  { lessons: [
    ['Model answer: conflict between engineers', '*Q: How did you handle conflict between two engineers?*\n*S:* Two senior engineers clashed over whether to rewrite a service; reviews had become hostile.\n*A:* I met each privately and found the real issue — unclear ownership of the service. I brought them together around our shared goal (cut latency by 50%), asked each to write a one-page proposal with trade-offs, and had the tech lead facilitate a design review. We picked an incremental refactor with clear owners for each part.\n*R:* Latency dropped 45% in a quarter, reviews became civil, and both later said the written proposals helped.\n*Reflection:* Unclear ownership causes many "personality" conflicts — I now set ownership explicitly in a team charter."'],
    ['Model answer: morale after layoffs', '*Q: How did you keep a team going after layoffs?*\n*Answer (summary):* "We lost 2 of 8 engineers. The same day I held a team meeting: I shared what I knew, admitted what I didn\'t, and let people talk. I held 1:1s with everyone that week. Then I went to my director with a reduced roadmap — we dropped one of three projects — so we weren\'t doing eight people\'s work with six. We set a no-weekend-work rule. Engagement scores dipped one quarter and then recovered, and we had no regretted departures in the next six months."'],
  ] },
  { lessons: [
    ['Model answer: pushing back on a senior stakeholder', '*Q: Tell me about pushing back on a senior leader.*\n*S:* Our VP asked us to build a new partner integration in six weeks for a sales deal.\n*T:* Our estimate was twelve weeks, and we were mid-way through a reliability project after two outages.\n*A:* I didn\'t say no. I prepared a one-page summary: the estimate with its assumptions, the risk of pausing reliability work, and three options — (1) a reduced integration covering the deal\'s must-haves in six weeks, (2) the full version in twelve weeks, (3) pause the reliability work and accept the outage risk. I pre-wired my director and the sales lead, then took it to the VP with my recommendation: option 1.\n*R:* The VP chose option 1, the deal closed, and the full version shipped the following quarter. The VP later asked me to use the same format for other trade-off decisions.\n*Reflection:* Options plus a recommendation turn a "no" into a decision."'],
    ['Model answer: disagreeing with your manager', '*Q: Tell me about disagreeing with your manager.*\n*Answer (summary):* "My director wanted to move to a single shared on-call rotation across four teams to save money. I thought it would slow down incident response. I pulled six months of incident data showing that 70% of incidents needed specific team knowledge, and proposed a pilot instead. She still wanted to try the shared rotation, so I committed fully and helped write the runbooks. After a month, response time had risen 40%, and we used the data to move to a hybrid model. Being data-driven and then committing kept our relationship strong."'],
  ] },
  { lessons: [
    ['Engineering budgets', 'Engineering budgets usually have:\n*People (opex)* — salaries, benefits, payroll taxes and bonuses; usually the biggest line. The *fully loaded cost* of an engineer is often about 1.25–1.4× base salary.\n*Cloud and infrastructure* — compute, storage, data transfer; tracked with FinOps practices (tagging, reserved or committed-use discounts, removing idle resources).\n*Tools and vendors* — SaaS licences, contractors, support contracts.\n*Other* — hiring costs, training and travel.\n*Capex vs opex*: some companies capitalise engineering time spent building long-lived software; finance decides the rules.\nBudget cycle: an annual plan built from the roadmap and headcount plan, then quarterly reforecasts. Know your numbers, explain variances early, and tie every request to business outcomes ("this $200k platform investment saves ~$350k a year in cloud costs and two engineers\' worth of toil").'],
    ['Budget questions and model answers', '*Q: How do you build a budget for your team?*\n*A:* "I start from the roadmap: what we must deliver and what that needs in people, infrastructure and tools. I use fully loaded cost per role, add cloud forecasts from current usage plus growth, and list vendors with renewal dates. I review it with finance and my director, then track actuals monthly and reforecast each quarter."\n*Q: You\'ve been asked to cut 15%. What do you do?*\n*A:* "First I\'d understand the goal and timeline. Then I\'d list options with their impact: cloud savings (rightsizing, committed-use discounts, deleting unused resources — often 10–20% of the cloud bill), consolidating overlapping tools, pausing backfills or contractors, and delaying a lower-priority project. I\'d recommend the mix that protects our top priorities and people where possible, be clear about what we\'ll stop doing, and communicate it honestly to the team."\n*Q: How do you justify headcount?*\n*A:* "I tie it to outcomes: the roadmap items we can\'t deliver without the role, the cost of delay, on-call load, and the risk of a low bus factor — with numbers."'],
    ['Model answer: your first 90 days', '*Q: What would your first 90 days look like here?*\n*Answer:* "*Days 1–30: listen and learn.* 1:1s with every report, my manager, my peers and key stakeholders; learn the systems, roadmap, metrics and pain points; read recent postmortems and survey results.\n*Days 31–60: quick wins and diagnosis.* Fix one or two visible pain points the team raised, such as flaky tests or noisy alerts; share what I\'ve learned and check it with the team.\n*Days 61–90: set direction.* Agree on team goals and a plan for the next quarter, clarify roles and expectations, and set up the rhythms I\'ll keep — 1:1s, planning and retros. I\'d review progress with my manager at each stage."'],
  ] },
];
